From 9e77c37a7d92c41da8967f9bc161fd225cae7beb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 09:09:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cursor: bill tool-loop segments from a run ledger so cached
 prompts show up
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Cursor reports usage exactly once per Agent run, in the TurnEnded that closes
it, and those counters are cumulative over every segment the run produced. One
gateway request drives one segment, so an agentic tool loop finished almost
every request with no usage at all: the response carried no usage chunk, the
statistics store fell back to an estimate of the whole prompt with zero cached
tokens, and the run's closing request then reported the entire run again.
Production logs show this directly — 66 of the 103 requests served in the hour
before this change ended on finish_reason=tool_calls with no usage anywhere in
the response, which is what surfaces as five-figure prompts with no cache. A
six-turn tool loop against the deployed gateway reported nothing at all on
every one of its turns.

The session now keeps a ledger for the run. Segments that end without a report
are billed from an estimate that counts the prefix the run already sent as a
cache read; the cumulative report is then reduced by whatever those estimates
covered, so a run still totals Cursor's own numbers. Every segment_end is now
preceded by a usage report, so the tool_calls responses that make up an agent
loop stop looking uncached. The same six-turn loop now reports ~95% cache on
each turn after the first.

Cursor's counters are not disjoint despite the Anthropic-style field names: a
turn_ended carrying input_tokens 18817 with cache_read_tokens 18717 and
cache_write_tokens 98 describes an ~18.8k prompt served almost entirely from
cache, not a 37.6k one. Keep input_tokens as the whole prompt with the cache
counters as subsets of it, which is already the OpenAI wire shape, and record
cache writes in the statistics detail instead of dropping them.

Co-authored-by: Apologetic2022 <Apologetic2022@users.noreply.github.com>
